\begin{abstract}
We present a complete, end-to-end architecture for a metric-wave continuum
computational substrate that implements digital logic using non-linear field
dynamics rather than conventional transistor-based gates. The system is built
from a spatially discretized continuum governed by a non-linear wave partial
differential equation (PDE), realized physically in a high-resistivity
Silicon-on-Insulator (HR-SOI) stack with integrated VO$_2$ non-linear mixing
nodes and absorbing Perfectly Matched Layer (PML) boundaries. A 1-bit full
adder cell is implemented via destructive interference (Sum) and cubic
third-harmonic generation (Carry), validated in simulation and mapped to a
fabrication-ready GDSII and mask stack. An autonomous hardware-in-the-loop
control loop using an RFSoC FPGA platform provides self-healing operation,
phase re-locking, and thermal stabilization, enabling robust computation in
the presence of noise. This document unifies the physics, device, cascade,
continuum, and platform layers into a single scientific specification.
\end{abstract}

